WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:05.440
Let’s say you really need
to find reliable information

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.640
about the best diet for
high blood pressure,

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.800
or heart disease,
or diabetes.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:12.160
Where do you go?

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.720
Do you go to a website
sponsored by Big Pharma

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.520
that wants to sell you
pills to fix your problem?

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:22.560
Or, do you want
to treat the cause?

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:26.080
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.600
with the latest peer-
reviewed research

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.400
on the best ways to eat
healthy and live longer.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.200
The average adult uses nine
personal care products a day

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.960
and each of these products can have
hundreds of chemical ingredients.

00:00:39.504 --> 00:00:44.240
And don't get me started on
mad cow disease intestines.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:45.920
Here’s our first story.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.200
The Food and Drug
Administration recently

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.000
reopened comments about
their policy of allowing some

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.920
intestines, but not others,
into the U.S. food supply.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.240
When the first few cases
of mad cow disease started

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:02.400
popping up, the FDA's gut
reaction was to ban all guts

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.320
from food and personal care
products, but in 2005,

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:10.880
USDA and FDA amended their draft
rule to "permit the use of the

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.680
entire small intestine
for human food"

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:17.760
if the last 80 uncoiled inches going
to the colon was removed.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.280
Since then, though, studies
have shown that infectious

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:25.440
mad cow prions can be found
throughout all parts of the

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:28.400
intestine, from the stomach
down to the cow's colon,

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.680
raising the question of
whether all entrails should

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.600
again be removed.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.880
The North American Meat
Association says, NO, wanting

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.520
to keep all cattle insides
inside the food supply,

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.680
similar to what we heard
from the CTFA,

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.960
the Cosmetic, Toiletry,
and Fragrance Association.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.000
They protested that
by banning from cosmetics

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.560
downer and dead
cattle, as well as brain,

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:56.640
skull, eyes, and spinal cords,
as well as intestines, and tonsils,

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.520
our nation's supply of cosmetics
could be put in jeopardy.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:05.920
There could be a tallow
shortage for soap, for example.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:10.720
The FDA may not realize that
cosmetics and personal care products

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.800
are a quarter trillion dollar
industry worldwide.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.720
In the end, the FDA
tentatively concluded that

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.480
intestines should continue
to be allowed in the food and

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:26.080
cosmetic supply, because only
trace amounts of infectivity

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.120
have been found throughout
the bowels of cattle,

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.560
a conclusion they have to
make since otherwise,

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.480
all meat would have
to banned as well,

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.160
because new research shows
that mad cow infectivity

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:41.840
is in the muscles too.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.760
And, not just the atypical
cases of BSE

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:48.400
like the last mad cow
found in California,

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:52.560
but now we know
the typical BSE as well,

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.680
Bovine Spongiform Encephalopathy -
mad cow disease.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:00.160
Low levels of infectious prions
found in the ribs, shoulders,

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.840
tenderloins, sirloin tips, and
round cuts of meat.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:09.440
The latest estimates out of
Britain suggest 15,000 people

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:13.200
are currently incubating the
human form of mad cow disease,

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.640
contracted through the
consumption of infected meat.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.280
Fewer than 200 Brits have
died so far of variant

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.880
Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, but
the incubation period for this

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.600
invariably fatal
neurodegenerative disease,

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:29.840
can be decades,

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:34.240
the time between eating the meat
and one's brain filling up with holes.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.880
The fact that so many people
are carrying it has important

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.760
implications for the safety of
blood transfusions-

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.240
that's why many Americans
who've lived in England

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:46.640
are barred by the Red Cross
from donating blood,

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.040
as well as the safety of
handling surgical instruments

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:53.760
that may have cut
into someone who's a carrier,

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:57.520
since it's so hard to sterilize
anything once contaminated.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:01.760
Given these factors, it may be
prudent to err on the side of

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:04.640
caution when regulating which
intestines are allowed

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:08.640
on and in our mouths.
But it's a balance.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:12.080
As one meat company points
out, guts are not just used

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:15.920
for lipstick, intestines is
human food, "providing us with

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:20.000
a precious source of protein
which is evidently essential

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.080
for our human population."

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.200
In our next story, dozens
of lipsticks and lip glosses

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:31.040
are put to the test
for lead contamination.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:33.760
Over the past years, the
use of cosmetic products

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:35.840
has evidently increased
at an alarming rate

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:39.440
due to unending pursuit for
individual beautification.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.960
Nothing necessarily wrong
with that unless cosmetic products

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.240
contained ingredients that
may be linked to disease.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.680
Ingredients, such as toxic
heavy metals like lead.

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:54.720
Lead has been found in a wide
range of cosmetic products,

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.360
from eye shadow to skin
cream, foundation, blush.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:01.280
I talked about henna before…
but looking at the data,

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:03.680
an important warning
can be recognized—

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:05.840
the presence of lead in lipsticks—

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:07.200
because you end up inadvertently

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.040
actually swallowing a
little bit of it.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:13.280
It has been estimated that a woman
may end up ingesting three pounds

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:15.520
of lipstick over her lifetime.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:18.800
Moreover, lipsticks can be
used by pregnant women

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:21.480
or women of child bearing age.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:22.720
Uh, duh.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:27.760
Yes, lead is highly toxic,
but how much lead

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:29.440
can there be in lipstick?

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:32.320
Surely, it’s a very minor source.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.040
Nonetheless, one should
not exclude the fact

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:37.120
that lead accumulates
in the body over time,

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:39.920
and so repetitive lead-
containing lipstick application

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:41.360
might lead to significant exposure.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:43.040
But, you don’t really know…

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:45.760
until you put it to the test.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:49.040
Thirty-two lipsticks and
lip glosses tested,

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:53.120
and lead was detected in
three-fourths of the products

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:54.960
suggesting public health concerns.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:57.520
But how much lead did they find?

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:01.760
About half exceeded the FDA-
recommended maximum level

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:05.120
set for candy.
Yeah, but come on.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:08.080
That limit is set for something
kids may eat every day.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:11.280
Kids are not going to eat
tubes of lipstick every day.

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:14.080
Nevertheless, it's generally accepted

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:17.040
that there's no safe
level of lead intake;

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:20.480
ideally, we should get
contaminant levels down to zero.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:23.120
And look, as a consumer
group pointed out,

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:25.040
a quarter of the lipsticks
were lead-free,

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:27.040
so we know it can be done.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:30.720
So, maybe we should better
regulate toxic metals

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:33.840
in cosmetics to protect women’s
health in the United States,

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:36.160
as has already been
done in Europe.

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:37.380
Fair enough.

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:42.160
The billion-dollar lipstick
industry, however, wasn’t happy.

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:45.120
In an article that tried
to downplay the risks,

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:48.640
the scientists-for-hire firm
that once played villain

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:50.880
in the real-life Erin Brockovich case,

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:53.760
concluded that although
lipstick may contain lead,

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:56.320
the concentrations are so
low that they would not

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:59.760
be expected to pose any health
risks to adults or children.

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:02.640
Children’s blood lead
levels are influenced more

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:06.560
by background lead exposures
(in the air/dust/water/food)

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:08.320
than by lipstick exposures.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:12.240
Ok, but just because our
environment is so contaminated

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.720
doesn’t mean we need
to add to the problem.

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:18.320
In fact, because there’s so
much lead around anyway,

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:20.240
maybe it’s that much more reason

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:22.320
to cut down on additional exposures,

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:26.400
but they calculate that an adult
would need to apply lipstick

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:29.600
over 30 times a day to raise
their blood lead level

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:31.360
to even the most stringent limits,

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:34.720
and 695 times a day
to get blood levels

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:36.640
up to more concerning levels.

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:41.280
Ah, but this was based on
an assumption that lipstick

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:43.760
would only have about one
part per million lead,

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:46.480
or at the extreme end
maybe two or three.

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:52.240
But by 2016, about ten times
more lipsticks were tested,

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:55.920
and they averaged nearly
500 parts per million,

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:58.800
with 10 percent over 1,000,
all the way up to 10,000

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:02.320
with more than one out
of five exceeding FDA

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:05.680
and even Chinese safety
limits on lead in cosmetics.

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:10.400
Lip gloss was worse than lipstick,
orange and pink had more lead

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:13.600
than brown, red, or purple,
and all the really contaminated

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:16.000
ones were the cheaper
ones, under five bucks.

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:20.560
But wait a second, 10,185 milligrams?

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:25.520
That’s 10 grams per kilogram,
which means the lipstick was

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:28.400
1 percent pure lead.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:32.400
That means a single application
could expose a grown woman

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:36.080
to perhaps 12 times
the tolerable daily intake.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:39.200
And if she’s interested
in having children,

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:41.120
then that poses a
particular concern,

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:42.880
as lead accumulates in your bones

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:45.680
and may then be released into
the bloodstream during pregnancy,

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:49.200
where it can slip through the
placenta or into the breastmilk.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:53.360
The good news is that the FDA
is considering lowering

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:56.880
the maximum allowable lead
levels in lipstick from 20 to 10,

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:59.200
something Canada arrived
at a decade ago.

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:02.960
But without enforcement,
it doesn’t matter.

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:05.440
Moving the legal limit
from 20 down to 10

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:08.160
would just mean that instead
of 23 percent of lip products

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:13.056
exceeding legal levels 27 percent
would be exceeding legal levels.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:15.760
Finally today, we ask,

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:19.120
is there risk of lead
and PPD contamination

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:20.776
of red and black henna?

00:09:21.591 --> 00:09:23.440
Over recent decades,
we've started to recognize

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:28.160
that some topically applied substances
can penetrate into or even through

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:31.520
human skin and end up
circulating throughout our bodies.

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:35.600
Take the toxic heavy metal
lead, for example.

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:38.800
To see if lead could be absorbed
through the skin into the body,

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:41.360
researchers applied lead
to someone's left arm,

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:44.800
and then they measured the level of lead
in the sweat coming off their right arm

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:47.040
over the next few days, and
there was a big spike,

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:51.920
proving, nearly 30 years ago, that lead
can be absorbed through the skin

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:54.320
and rapidly distributed
throughout the body.

00:09:55.280 --> 00:09:57.840
This led public health authorities
to recommend parents

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:02.000
avoid using cosmetics, at least on their
children, that could be contaminated.

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:04.480
Which cosmetics might those be?

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:09.280
Lead has been found in a wide
range of cosmetic products

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:12.720
because it's a natural constituent
of many color pigments.

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:17.040
The FDA has set an upper limit
for lead at 20 parts per million,

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:20.960
and though only some samples
of henna exceeded that—

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:23.200
because henna is used
for temporary tattoos—

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:26.400
these quantities of lead can
remain on the skin for a long time

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:28.960
and may not be safe.

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:34.640
Because studies show that lead may
have no identifiable safe exposure level

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:38.400
with even the lowest levels shown to
affect the brains of developing children,

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:41.440
thus the use of henna,
especially among children,

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:43.440
may constitute a public health risk.

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:48.480
So increasing awareness of henna's
serious toxic implications may help

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:51.440
end or at least reduce the use
of such hazardous materials,

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:54.480
especially when children are involved.

00:10:55.600 --> 00:11:00.560
Now traditionally, henna was just the
dried powdered leaves of some plant.

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:05.280
But more recently, other ingredients have
been added to give it a stronger color,

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:07.920
added ingredients such as lead,

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:12.160
one of the most common and
egregious additives in henna.

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:17.520
But not as common as PPD,
para-phenylene-diamine.

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:21.840
The red paste traditionally
used, known as "red henna,"

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:23.520
rarely produces adverse effects.

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:26.880
But to achieve a darker pigment,
known as "black henna,"

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:30.320
various additives may be
used including animal urine,

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:36.880
but better pee than PPD,
a coal tar derivative that can cause

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:40.000
nasty skin reactions such
as blistering and scarring.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:45.200
PPD is added to speed up the
process from as long as 12 hours,

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:46.640
down to less than 2 hours.

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:48.800
So while the use of black
hennas may be tempting,

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:52.240
it has the potential for both short-
and long-term side effects.

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:55.120
How common are these reactions?

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:57.440
The best estimate is about 2.5%,

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:03.360
so 1 in 40 kids that gets a black henna
tattoo may have an allergic reaction.

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:08.000
Unfortunately, this practice has become
fashionable (thanks a lot, Spice Girls).

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:12.000
There's no such thing
as natural black henna,

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:15.440
so perhaps it would best to respect
the more traditional practices,

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:19.120
lest a temporary tattoo
turns into a permanent scar.

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:24.720
The problem is that PPD can be found
in products labeled as red henna too,

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:27.440
so just because it's red
doesn't mean it's not risky.

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:31.360
Bad news for the $100 million industry.

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:35.360
Because henna of all colors
is so often adulterated,

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:40.811
under FDA guidelines, henna
shouldn't be applied to the skin at all.

